 "report": "Particle A42-spiral-e000-d042: knot (k=6)\n\nWhat it is: a particle of mass 2.2 and radius 1.52 with 6 ports on its rim. Each port is a stick-slip contact with a dead zone (mean 0.27) and a 4-dim charge; 1 of its port pairs attract each other strongly (so copies can bind port to port). Inside, 14 couplings (29% inhibitory) pass a push from port to port; it settles in 4 sweeps with slip rate 0.97 and keeps its state (memory).\nHow it assembles (32 copies, 2 worlds): 0.38 bonds per particle, largest molecule 2.5 particles, 20.5 left free.\n\nIts matter vs an ESN of the same size (readout only, both with tuned input scale); controls: no bonds, random bonds:\n  memcap   0.113 vs ESN 0.999\u00b10.000  [below]  no bonds 0.112  random bonds 0.111\n  xor      0.581 vs ESN 0.529\u00b10.011  [beat]  no bonds 0.574  random bonds 0.480\n  narma10  0.275 vs ESN 0.841\u00b10.002  [below]  no bonds 0.273  random bonds 0.280\n  latch    0.783 vs ESN 0.679\u00b10.009  [beat]  no bonds 0.784  random bonds 0.786\n  mnist14  0.491 vs ESN 0.816\u00b10.007  [below]  no bonds 0.462  random bonds 0.471\n\nGood at: xor, latch.\nIts assembly does not matter: the particles do as well unbonded, so the useful part is the single particle's own stick-slip memory.\nUse (a guess): combining recent events nonlinearly (temporal feature detection); holding a value until it is overwritten, with nothing trained but the readout: a physical memory element for a recurrent or reservoir system."
